\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Evaluate the extent to which human rights are more effectively protected by humanitarian intervention than by international courts \par
\par
 \par
\par
Human rights are the rights guaranteed to all people; they are most widely understood as the four freedoms: freedom of speech and expression, freedom of worship, freedom from want, and freedom from fear. Humanitarian interventions are the use of military force with the intent of protecting human rights. International courts seek to prosecute countries or individuals on the basis of international law, and examples include the International Criminal Court or the International Court of Justice. It will firstly be argued that due to the now multipolar state of the world, interventions are very difficult, and thus courts work better to protect rights. Secondly, it will be argued that despite the legitimacy of courts, their decisions are rarely enforced, and thus the direct approach of intervention is better at protecting rights. Lastly, it will be argued that courts are poor at protecting human rights due to their retroactive nature, and the proactive nature of intervention protects more people's rights. Excellent intro \f1\endash  very clear; good judgements and great outline.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
It could be argued that the post-Cold War period was an era of optimism and was dominated by a hegemonic United States that prioritised intervention >> this point sentence must include the words of the question; show your focus. The unrivalled military and political power of the United States during this period and the election of proactive leaders such as Tony Blair led to an era of intervention. For example, the British intervention in Sierra Leone in 2002 incorrect year was highly effective and widely regarded as successful. Additionally, the troops of the British were rapidly undeployed and thus did not stir up unnecessary negativity from the local population Accuracy \endash  actually, didn\rquote t some UK forces stay, to train up the local police? So you are right that they didn\rquote t stir up negativity, but the reason you give doesn\rquote t quite reflect what happened. The NATO intervention in Kosovo in 1999, despite not gaining UNSC approval, was still able to take place and was successful in swiftly ending the war. The lack of UN approval further proves the importance of a strong hegemon (the United States) in leading to successful interventions. These examples show cases where strong powers and a unipolar world order led to successful and fast interventions. However, it can be more convincingly argued that the rise of a more multipolar world has led to interventions being much less likely to succeed. The growing relevance of China and Russia has led to fewer interventions being able to take place; their use of P5 veto powers has blocked many interventions in places such as Myanmar. Resolutions concerning Myanmar are consistently vetoed by China, preventing all action. During this era of multipolarity, courts and tribunals are still highly active and productive. Despite the deep humanitarian concerns in Syria during its prolonged conflict, there have been no interventions due to its status as a Russian ally and Russia\rquote s sway over the security council and thus the UN. However, there is a current arrest warrant for Putin issued by the ICC since the 17th of March 2023. This demonstrates that even as the global order shifts, courts are continuously effective at protecting human rights. This paragraph is overall good, with some great stuff on interventions, but there is very little on courts give that the point of the essay is to compare courts/tribunals and interventions \endash  your coverage in each paragraph needs to balance both aspects of the question, so you are able to make effective comparisons and therefore effective judgements about which is more effective at protecting rights.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
It could be argued that international courts are more effective at protecting human rights due to their strong legitimacy. International courts such as the ICC and the ICJ prosecute on the basis of international law and support from the international community. The Rome Statute (the document that brought the ICC into existence) was signed by 137 << I don\rquote t think this number is accurate? Where did you find this? countries, which is a significant proportion of UN-recognised states. Due to this, the ICC can claim legitimacy as a body that is widely recognised by states as an international justice keeper. The ICC has used its legitimacy to prosecute several figures who have endangered human rights, such as Joseph Kony, founder of the Lord\rquote s Resistance Army in Uganda. The court also sought to prosecute Muammar Gaddafi of Libya before his death in 2011. The court has handled 32 cases since its establishment in 2002. These cases show the ICC (an important international court) using its legitimacy to prosecute people who endanger human rights. However, it can be more convincingly argued that despite a less apparent legitimacy, humanitarian intervention more often succeeds. The ICC warrants for Vladimir Putin, Benjamin Netanyahu, Gaddafi, and Kony are still outstanding. In fact, to resolve the situation in Libya, instead of following the ICC\rquote s arrest warrant, a NATO-led military coalition killed Gaddafi, hoping to end his despotic reign. While not all interventions are fully supported by the global community, and this goes against the liberal principles of a body like the UN or the ICC, interventions are more frequently effective. Realists would argue that states are only likely to be coerced by force and be unwilling to collaborate, and this is shown by countries unwillingness to enforce ICC arrest warrants, such as when Putin visited South Africa. Interventions are more likely to be effective in an anarchistic world order due to their forceful nature when compared to the collaborative nature of international courts. Again, this is in many ways really good, with great examples and good explanation of those examples, as well as good evaluation and judgement \endash  but, you have not really covered both sides of the question, because you have only very briefly mentioned interventions. Your comparative reference to NATO in Libya is very effective, but you should be doing a lot more of this.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
It could be argued that international courts are more able to protect human rights as they retroactively enforce justice upon perpetrators. International tribunals such as the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia, set up by the UNSC, were established to retroactively prosecute those who had committed great crimes and infringed upon human rights. Former Yugoslavian president Slobodan Milosevic was tried by this body. Despite his death, he would be implicated as guilty in later trials. This example demonstrates the power of international courts to prevent further human rights abuses by setting precedent and removing violators of human rights from power. Here, for example, you could compare this to the swift and effective interventions in Bosnia (1995) and Kosovo (1999), so you are making more effective direct comparisons between both aspects of the question. Many other people were tried and convicted by this tribunal, which lasted between 1993 and 2017. This, among other tribunals established by the UN, could effectively bring justice to many. However, it could be argued that international trials through tribunals or previously established courts fail to protect human rights as they are retroactive. By the end of the Bosnian genocide during the Yugoslav wars, around 40,000 people had already been killed. This questions the effectiveness of retroactive international justice, as it can be seen as acting too late by the time so many people had already been killed by human rights abusers. The human rights of many were failed to be protected in time by international courts. INTERFET The peacekeeping mission authorised by the UNSC in East Timor was successfully able to restore order away from pro-Indonesian militias and bring peace and eventually sovereignty to the region. This occurred before the situation became dire and expanded to destabilise the region entirely. While not all interventions are so successful, the proactive nature of interventions gives them a higher chance of preventing human rights abuses and thus protecting human rights. Inherently, interventions are able to prevent the escalation of abuses and thus protect human rights better. Good use of East Timor \par
\par
In conclusion, it is clear that humanitarian intervention is better at protecting human rights than international courts. Despite the multipolar world we now live in, other factors give interventions the advantage to protect rights. Despite the legitimacy of courts, their decisions are rarely upheld in an anarchistic world order, and interventions with forceful methods are more likely to effectively protect human rights abuses. Lastly, the retroactive nature of courts means they fail to prevent human rights abuses as they happen, and thus interventions protect human rights better. Overall, interventions more successfully protect human rights. Good, clear judgement \par
